2026-10-08|Maximum Frequency After At Most K Increments

Question
Given an integer array `arr[]`, in one operation we can choose an index and increase its value by `1`.
Find the maximum possible frequency of any element after performing at most `k` operations.

Approach
1. Sort the array.

2. Maintain a sliding window `[left, right]`.

3. Treat `arr[right]` as the value we want every element in the current window to become.

4. The number of operations needed is:
   `arr[right] * windowSize - sum`

5. If this cost is at most `k`, the entire window can be converted to `arr[right]`, so update the answer.

6. If the cost exceeds `k`, move `left` forward and subtract `arr[left]` from the window sum until the window becomes affordable again.

7. Continue this process for every `right`.

8. Sorting ensures that we only need to increase smaller values toward the current maximum. We never need to decrease any value.

9. Time complexity is O(n log n) because of sorting, followed by O(n) sliding-window processing.

10. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space apart from the sorting implementation.

Solution
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int maxFrequency(vector<int>& arr, long long k) {
    sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());

    int left = 0;
    long long sum = 0;
    int ans = 1;

    for (int right = 0; right < arr.size(); right++) {
        sum += arr[right];

        while (1LL * arr[right] * (right - left + 1) - sum > k) {
            sum -= arr[left];
            left++;
        }

        ans = max(ans, right - left + 1);
    }

    return ans;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    long long k;

    cin >> n >> k;

    vector<int> arr(n);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cin >> arr[i];
    }

    cout << maxFrequency(arr, k) << endl;

    return 0;
}
